        {
          "unit_id": "artifact_001_unit_012",
          "unit_type": "lean_theorem",
          "label": "ex1",
          "source_range": {
            "start_line": 92,
            "end_line": 100
          },
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "block_id": "artifact_001_unit_012_statement",
              "block_type": "formal_statement",
              "content": "theorem ex1 : ∀ x, ¬ provable (conj x False) ∧ (provable x → ¬ provable False)",
              "language": "lean",
              "source_range": {
                "start_line": 92,
                "end_line": 92
              }
            },
            {
              "block_id": "artifact_001_unit_012_proof",
              "block_type": "proof_body",
              "content": "by\n    intro x\n    constructor\n    · intro h\n      have pf : provable False := AxConjElimRight x False h\n      exact AxNotPrFalse pf\n    · intros px pf\n      exact AxNotPrFalse pf",
              "language": "lean",
              "source_range": {
                "start_line": 93,
                "end_line": 100
              }
            },
            {
              "block_id": "artifact_001_unit_012_explanation",
              "block_type": "explanation",
              "content": "Explanation for ex1:\nThis theorem shows two ideas:\n1. The conjunction (x ∧ False) is not provable, because it would lead to provable False.\n2. Even if x is provable, that does not mean False is provable.\n\nThe proof uses:\n- Elimination on conj x False to get provable False.\n- Then applies AxNotPrFalse to derive a contradiction.\n- So the whole conjunction is unprovable, and provable x does not allow proving False.",
              "language": "en",
              "source_range": {
                "start_line": 131,
                "end_line": 139
              }
            }
          ],
